well i have the honor of talking with  nadia murad nobel peace prize laureate  and founder of nadia's initiative which  was launched after the violent  persecution of the yazidi people in iraq  at the hands of isis some eight years  ago tragically this also included your  own kidnapping and persecution and  suffering at the hands of isis  now you were awarded the nobel peace  prize for your work to fight against the  use of sexual violence as a tactic of  war used against women and children in  2018  now we're hearing horrible reports of  russian soldiers usually using sexual  violence against women and children in  ukraine for yourself when you hear this  kind of news what are your thoughts what  are your feelings  you know  it is heartbreaking uh for me as a  survivor of sexual violence and a  genocide to see that  what happened to me in my community in  2014 is happening right now to  thousands of people in ukraine  i have been watching the news very  closely the the reports of sexual  violence and  you know and we know that whenever a  conflict  can't break out we see that the sexual  violence follows and and we are seeing  that in ukraine right now but  i am i'm glad that the international  community is uh  seems more united more than ever to  address the the use of sexual violence  at a very you know an early  you know quickly because in in the past  and and from my experience in when isis  what isis did to us  uh it was sexual violence was not in the  international community's agenda when  they you know talk about war and  conflict  but this time we're seeing more leaders  more politicians more  even the media are talking about it and  reporting and listening to survivors in  ukraine  well we know that last month you  publicly unveiled  murad's code which was a supposed to be  an international guideline  for  helping i guess leadership country  leadership  learn to  support  survivors of sexual assault and help  them seek justice which in some ways  couldn't be better timing considering  what is going on right now in ukraine  why did you feel it was necessary to  develop this code and what do you hope  to accomplish through it  you know i've been working with the uk  government recently and we we were able  to launch the morale code last month in  in  at the united nations  uh you know survivors of  i'm telling you from a personal  experience in in idp camps when  there were thousands of yezidi survivors  telling their stories and and  testimonies but they were not  you know they they were not  told that how their stories and their  testimonies their photos will be used or  will not you know that the  the  likely outcomes of of their testimonies  uh there are you know  the hope of like doing this code is that  to make sure that journalists  investigators and anyone who is engaging  working with survivors they will do it  in a supportive and respectful way  because survivors they are ready to tell  us their stories they are the one that  went through everything and we need  their uh testimonies as an uh evidence  to  use against the abuser the perpetrators  to hold them accountable so it is so  important to make sure that they are  safe and they they have all the support  that they need and we already we have  translated to  the ukraine and ukrainian language and  we shared with the government and with  the survivors so that everyone can can  follow that  the guidelines  well talking about justice and  accountability we know that recently a  german court convicted a member of isis  for genocide  what does that mean for you and for your  work knowing that that conviction came  through  you know  for me for my work and for every  survivors you know everyone  every survivor deserves uh their day in  court and the the what what that isis  member did to the uh to the community  and to this that specific  child and the women it was something  public and  uh  you know the german government  they they showed that  there is no impunity from such crimes  and there is no culture of impunity from  you know  committing genocide and sexual violence  and we're working to make other european  countries to use that you know germans  example and follow their example to  hold more perpetrators accountable this  is just one case we have thousands of  testimonies and evidence documents from  the united and from survivors so we are  hoping that we can see more cases like  this because there is there is no  healing without justice  especially for survivors yeah  and we find ourselves here at the world  economic forum this week where world  leaders will be discussing of course the  war in ukraine the pandemic rising  consumer prices where do you see  your initiative fitting in what are your  expectations for davos this week  you know i'm here uh to make sure that  the topic of conflict-related sexual  violence is something within their  agenda uh there are leaders here  politicians ngos uh  and and we are hoping that we can  address this thing we can see it as a  not just as a side effect of of conflict  sexual violence is being used as a  weapon of war throughout the history and  uh we are hoping that hunadies  initiative the global survivors fund  that i co-founded with my uh friend dr  dennis mccoy  to  make sure that we support survivors we  don't forget them because there are so  many things going on in the world and  the in the world leaders and and the  media move on very fast but  for survivors it's not that easy to just  move on and and it is so important to  support them through operations through  justice accountability and to listen to  them and to make sure that  we our voice is is hurt here too by  leaders and and  everyone here  well there's obviously a lot of work  involved being an activist against  sexual violence as you are what  in your mind is one of the biggest  hurdles or frustrations that you face  you know in in our case in yezidi's case  in in our work in sinjar it there are so  many challenges there are some political  challenges for example right now in  sinjar as you said at the beginning it's  been eight years since isis committed a  genocide against the yezidis and we we  still have thousands of people in  displacement camps only two hours away  from their homeland the international  community failed to protect us in 2014  but they also have failed to help people  to go back to their homeland and help  them to have access to  proper education health care and and and  you know by just basic services there  are thousands of women and girls who are  in displacement  camps and they don't have basic privacy  there are there's child marriage there  is suicide and there are so many  challenges but the biggest one is that  you know we are it's so hard to convince  the governments ngos to focus on  sustainable solutions after that the  conflict is end and i think we cannot  help people to fully recover from the  from the trauma if we don't focus on  sustainable solutions in their own  homeland and make sure that they have a  dignified life after what they've been  through  how has the pandemic affected your work  you know like everyone's else it was not  easy for for our work  our work is not like a business to do it  through you know zoom and other uh  online um meetings it was hard to  explain it i this work needs to you know  go to people go to students leaders ngos  and talk to them for hours to to tell  them about what is what is happening to  tell them about the ongoing needs for  for survivors to to be able to heal and  recover  uh it  it was not easy for us and that is why  right now we are trying to go to  everywhere and and and do what we can to  help  survivors in iraq in ukraine in in  ethiopia and yemen in other places  but  it it it was not easy and i'm i'm trying  my best to to make sure that  uh  that we still have the the  the media's and the world leaders  attention on on the topic of conflict  related sexual violence and the  aftermath of genocide and how we can  help communities to thrive again and  through our work yeah  nadia murad thank you so much and best  of luck with your work thank you so much

